1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,442
Methods package behavior. Arrays package many values.

2
00:00:04,442 --> 00:00:10,727
An array is fixed-size, indexed, and homogeneous — same type in every slot.

3
00:00:10,727 --> 00:00:16,761
Arrays are objects in Java, with special syntax and fast indexed access.

4
00:00:16,761 --> 00:00:22,376
They underpin collections, buffers, and performance-sensitive code.

5
00:00:22,376 --> 00:00:26,650
Get the mental model right — index, length, bounds.

6
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:28,071
Episode Eight.

7
00:00:28,071 --> 00:00:33,956
Arrays — fixed size, indexed access, and off-by-one traps.

8
00:00:33,956 --> 00:00:36,390
Declaration looks like this.

9
00:00:36,390 --> 00:00:39,347
int scores equals new int of five.

10
00:00:39,347 --> 00:00:43,868
Length is five. Valid indices are zero through four.

11
00:00:43,868 --> 00:00:49,693
Remember — zero-based indexing. The last index is length minus one.

12
00:00:49,693 --> 00:00:55,431
Once created, the length never grows. Fixed size means fixed size.

13
00:00:55,431 --> 00:01:00,822
If you need growth — that is a list conversation, coming soon.

14
00:01:00,822 --> 00:01:02,445
Access is by index.

15
00:01:02,445 --> 00:01:08,166
scores bracket zero equals ninety. Read and write in constant time.

16
00:01:08,166 --> 00:01:13,375
Ask for scores bracket five — ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

17
00:01:13,375 --> 00:01:20,719
Off-by-one bugs love loops that use less-than-or-equal when they should use less-than.

18
00:01:20,719 --> 00:01:25,672
Practice saying length minus one out loud until it sticks.

19
00:01:25,672 --> 00:01:29,501
Multidimensional arrays are arrays of arrays.

20
00:01:29,501 --> 00:01:34,522
int bracket bracket grid — rows that each hold a row array.

21
00:01:34,522 --> 00:01:39,032
Rows can even have different lengths — jagged arrays.

22
00:01:39,032 --> 00:01:44,563
Do not assume one flat contiguous block the way C sometimes does.

23
00:01:44,563 --> 00:01:50,690
When you need a true matrix library — use a library. Arrays stay simple.

24
00:01:50,690 --> 00:01:54,327
When do you choose arrays versus ArrayList?

25
00:01:54,327 --> 00:01:58,894
Arrays — fixed size, simple, very fast indexed access.

26
00:01:58,894 --> 00:02:04,391
ArrayList — grows, richer API, clearer for most application code.

27
00:02:04,391 --> 00:02:10,058
Prefer collections when intent is a growing list of domain objects.

28
00:02:10,058 --> 00:02:15,640
Prefer arrays for tight buffers, primitives, and interoperability.

29
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:20,292
Choose the structure that matches how the size changes.

30
00:02:20,292 --> 00:02:22,137
Three common mistakes.

31
00:02:22,137 --> 00:02:25,072
One — off-by-one indexing in loops.

32
00:02:25,072 --> 00:02:31,614
Two — exposing an internal array from a getter — callers can mutate your guts.

33
00:02:31,614 --> 00:02:37,568
Three — returning huge arrays from APIs when a stream or page would do.

34
00:02:37,568 --> 00:02:44,110
Also — using Object arrays when a typed collection communicates intent better.

35
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:48,052
Interview question — are arrays objects in Java?

36
00:02:48,052 --> 00:02:52,323
Yes — they live on the heap and have a length field.

37
00:02:52,323 --> 00:02:58,648
But they have special syntax — brackets — and covariant quirks to know later.

38
00:02:58,648 --> 00:03:03,247
Length for arrays. Size for lists. Do not mix the words.

39
00:03:03,247 --> 00:03:06,368
Zero-based indexing is non-negotiable.

40
00:03:06,368 --> 00:03:10,258
Many values, fixed slots. Next — text.

41
00:03:10,258 --> 00:03:12,613
Episode Nine — Strings.

42
00:03:12,613 --> 00:03:17,425
Immutability, equals, and careful construction.

43
00:03:17,425 --> 00:03:18,858
See you there.
